## Proposal

### What is the root cause of that problem?

The "Go back to home page" link calls `Navigation.goBack()` with no fallback route:

https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/5ca1a684b4d9e9cbf9c5129c40496a002d24e8b0/src/components/ValidateCode/ValidateCodeModal.tsx#L49-L51

`goBack()` gives up and returns without navigating when nothing can be popped and no fallback route was passed:

https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/5ca1a684b4d9e9cbf9c5129c40496a002d24e8b0/src/libs/Navigation/Navigation.ts#L560-L563

That branch is only reached on a route that is the *only* entry in the stack. Deep links normally get a full screen route prepended underneath them, so `canGoBack()` is true — except for the public link-entry screens, which are deliberately left standing alone (`/v/`, `/u/`, `/transition`, SAML, connection-complete):

https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/5ca1a684b4d9e9cbf9c5129c40496a002d24e8b0/src/libs/Navigation/helpers/getAdaptedStateFromPath.ts#L56-L63

But the real root cause is not this one override. `goBack()` used to handle "nothing to go back to" by sending the user to the app root. The original implementation called `openDrawer()` in exactly this branch, and later took a `shouldOpenDrawer` flag that defaulted to `true`:

https://github.com/Expensify/App/commit/3d9e274db7ac643be8b837a500eddd582df1a4ff

That fallback was deleted along with the drawer itself, and nothing replaced it — the branch became a bare `return`:

https://github.com/Expensify/App/commit/8ec253d42509ea2fa2136c506818d82bb3694671

So since 2023, every history-less `goBack()` has silently done nothing, and we have been fixing the same bug one page at a time:

- **#90929** — "request one here" on the expired magic-code page did nothing in a fresh tab. Fixed in **#96502** by resetting the stack to `TAB_NAVIGATOR`, on this very page:

https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/5ca1a684b4d9e9cbf9c5129c40496a002d24e8b0/src/components/ValidateCode/ExpiredValidateCodeModal.tsx#L49-L53

- The 2FA hand-off on the same `/v/` route needed its own copy of that workaround, with the same explanatory comment:

https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/5ca1a684b4d9e9cbf9c5129c40496a002d24e8b0/src/pages/ValidateLoginPage/index.web.tsx#L123-L134

- **This issue** is the third occurrence on the same route, and `UnlinkLoginPage` still has a bare `goBack()` on `/u/` that has never been looked at.

Separately, `NotFoundPage`, `SessionExpiredPage` and `LogOutPreviousUserPage` all pass `ROUTES.HOME` by hand for the same reason. Callers keep re-supplying a fallback that `goBack()` used to provide itself.


### What changes do you think we should make in order to solve the problem?

Restore the behaviour that was lost, in `goBack()` rather than in each page.

In the no-history branch, instead of returning immediately, reset the root stack to `NAVIGATORS.TAB_NAVIGATOR`. That is the modern equivalent of the drawer root: it is the tab navigator in `AuthScreens` and hosts `SignInPage` in `PublicScreens`, so it resolves whether or not the user is signed in — which is why the #90929 fix already chose that target.

Mirror the original opt-out too: add `shouldFallbackToRoot` to `GoBackOptions`, defaulting to `true`, so a caller that must stay put can say so. This is the same shape as the old `shouldOpenDrawer = true` parameter.

One guard is needed that the drawer version did not need. `canGoBack()` is also false when the user is legitimately at the app root, and re-opening a drawer was harmless where a reset is not — it would discard the nested tab state. So skip the fallback when the root state is already a single `TAB_NAVIGATOR` route, and keep today's no-op there. The fallback then only fires when the user is genuinely stranded on a route with nothing beneath it.

This fixes the reported link, the narrow-layout header back button on the same page (which inherits the same fallback-less default), `/u/`, and any future link-entry route, without touching any page.

<details>
<summary>Code suggestion</summary>

In `src/libs/Navigation/Navigation.ts` — add the option:

```ts
type GoBackOptions = {
    // ...existing options
    /**
     * When there is no history to pop, fall back to the app root instead of doing nothing. Mirrors the historical
     * `shouldOpenDrawer` flag this function used to take. Pass `false` for callers that must stay put when stranded.
     */
    shouldFallbackToRoot?: boolean;
};

const defaultGoBackOptions: Required<Pick<GoBackOptions, 'compareParams' | 'waitForTransition' | 'shouldFallbackToRoot'>> = {
    compareParams: true,
    waitForTransition: false,
    shouldFallbackToRoot: true,
};
```

and replace the no-history branch inside `goBack()`:

```ts
if (!backToRoute && !shouldPopToSidebar && !navigationRef.current?.canGoBack()) {
    const rootState = navigationRef.current?.getRootState();
    const isAlreadyAtRoot = rootState?.routes.length === 1 && rootState.routes.at(0)?.name === NAVIGATORS.TAB_NAVIGATOR;

    // Already at the root — resetting would only discard the nested tab state, so keep the no-op there.
    if (!(options?.shouldFallbackToRoot ?? defaultGoBackOptions.shouldFallbackToRoot) || isAlreadyAtRoot) {
        Log.hmmm('[Navigation] Unable to go back');
        return;
    }

    Log.hmmm('[Navigation] Unable to go back, falling back to the app root');
    navigationRef.current?.reset({index: 0, routes: [{name: NAVIGATORS.TAB_NAVIGATOR}]});
    return;
}
```

`NAVIGATORS` is already imported in this file.

</details>



*Note: `goBack()` is called throughout the app, so this deserves navigation-owner review and testing on native, where the hardware back button routes through the same function. The change is narrow in practice — only routes with nothing prepended beneath them reach this branch at all.*

### What alternative solutions did you explore? (Optional)

- **Remove the `onLinkPress` override in `ValidateCodeModal` only.** This does fix the reported button, and it is the smallest possible change. I did not choose it because it is the fourth local patch for one shared defect, and it leaves the header back button on the same page, `/u/`, and every future link-entry route broken. The proposals on this issue are split between this and a local `navigationRef.reset`, which is the same trade-off.
- **Give the whole app a Home fallback with no opt-out and no already-at-root guard.** Closest to the 2021 code, but a reset when the user is already home would throw away which tab they were on, and callers that intentionally rely on the no-op would have no way to keep it.
- **Prepend a full screen route under the public screens in `getAdaptedStateFromPath`.** This would make `canGoBack()` true everywhere and needs no `goBack()` change, but it reverts a guard that exists on purpose (`TabNavigator` does not exist for unauthenticated users), affecting every `/v/`, `/u/` and SAML deep link. Too risky for this.

